{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-218724",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Each dimension is given to the nearest $0.1\\,\\text{m}.$ So, the greatest possible error of these measurements is\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.1\\,\\text{m}}{2}=0.05\\,\\text{m}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the *upper bound* for the volume of the package. Therefore, we require the *upper bound* of each dimension.\n\nThe upper bounds for each dimension are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.9\\,\\text{m}+0.05\\,\\text{m}=0.95\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 0.9\\,\\text{m}+0.05\\,\\text{m}=0.95\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & 1.2\\,\\text{m}+0.05\\,\\text{m}=1.25\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the upper bound for the volume of the package is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =0.95\\,\\text{m}\\times 0.95\\,\\text{m}\\times 1.25\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =1.128\\,125\\,{\\text{m}}^{3} \\\\  & ≈1.1\\,{\\text{m}}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth of a cubic meter.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
